{"author":{"id":"2f242a7e7f2592f8909954a77fabece74f6e8cc197182647ed3b15ea8e3878af"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791224617,"nonce":"ixWRwDpEkUCiY5sFcljjAWcQO8c=","title":"a macOS wait can block forever","message":"Reported by ashui-8b (ashui git-bug 23ea6bb80593e8a14458f0ed80bb7f78a59dedec5d9290bcb68c0d5a62fc3bbb). ashui's window test hung in window.wait(\u003c=0.1) for 30 minutes at 0.2% CPU, sampled inside -[NSApplication run] -\u003e _DPSNextEvent -\u003e mach_msg. It hung in 2 of 3 runs, more often with the window behind the IDE.\n\nCause: a timed wait ran winit's pump untimed and woke it with a one-shot alarm. winit then ends -[NSApplication run] by posting a stop event from its after-waiting observer, and AppKit can miss an event posted while the run loop sleeps until the display cycle, which a covered window may not get. Events posted from another thread go unseen the same way (tested: a zero pump on 8d581e7 that spins until a frame isn't ended early by a background-thread post).\n\nFix: each run of the application starts on a serve event posted before it, which AppKit sees, and a local event monitor serves the run itself. It calls nextEventMatchingMask:untilDate:...dequeue:YES and hands each event to sendEvent: (winit's handler is set), and that call returns at its deadline whether or not an event comes. It drops serve events and winit's stop events (application-defined, subtype 0), returns once it has handled anything, then stops the application. winit's pump is now always untimed, so its before-waiting observer never posts.\n\nAfter (pollbench, window covered by the IDE): wait 0.1: worst lateness 4-6 ms, 2% CPU. wait 0.008: 104/s, worst 4 ms, 11% CPU (was 3-4% with the alarm; the fetch costs more per call). wgpu loop: 462 paced, 467 polling only. hlwindow gpu_test native: 450 frames.","files":null}]}